From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dugs <dugahol@protonmail.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 01:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: apple: Do not arm a masked interrupt in set_type

The pin mode field doubles as the interrupt mask (IN_IRQ_OFF), but
apple_gpio_irq_set_type() wrote the trigger mode unconditionally. A
type change on a masked or disabled interrupt (irq mapping, request_irq
with IRQF_NO_AUTOEN, irq_set_irq_type() on a disabled line) therefore
armed the pin in hardware while the IRQ core still considered it masked.
mask_irq() then skips the hardware for that interrupt, and with no
irq_mask_ack callback handle_level_irq() only acks it, so an asserted
level interrupt re-fires forever and wedges the CPU servicing the bank.

Seen on the iPhone 8 Plus touchscreen: after a module reload the touch
IRQ pin was in group 0 with mode=3 while core_masked=1, and the system
hung when the controller powered up and pulled the line low.

Only write the mode when the interrupt is unmasked; unmask applies the
stored trigger type.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dugs <dugahol@protonmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-apple-gpio.c b/drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-apple-gpio.c
index cc515c1..5a31235 100644
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-apple-gpio.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-apple-gpio.c
@@ -354,8 +354,17 @@ static int apple_gpio_irq_set_type(struct irq_data *data, unsigned int type)
 	if (irqtype == REG_GPIOx_IN_IRQ_OFF)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
-	apple_gpio_set_reg(pctl, data->hwirq, REG_GPIOx_MODE,
-			   FIELD_PREP(REG_GPIOx_MODE, irqtype));
+	/*
+	 * The pin's mode field is also its interrupt mask: IN_IRQ_OFF is the
+	 * masked state. Writing the trigger mode into a masked pin would arm it
+	 * behind the IRQ core's back, and since mask_irq() skips the hardware
+	 * for an interrupt it already believes masked, a level interrupt then
+	 * fires forever. Only arm the pin when it is unmasked;
+	 * apple_gpio_irq_unmask() applies the stored trigger type otherwise.
+	 */
+	if (!irqd_irq_masked(data))
+		apple_gpio_set_reg(pctl, data->hwirq, REG_GPIOx_MODE,
+				   FIELD_PREP(REG_GPIOx_MODE, irqtype));
 
 	if (type & IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_MASK)
 		irq_set_handler_locked(data, handle_level_irq);
